WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:07.040 position:63%
On May 20, 2026, 43,000 listings in Chicago&nbsp;
disappeared. All because Zillow refused to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.760 position:63%
list just… 9 of them. This is a small battle&nbsp;
in the war for the housing market. On each side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.600 position:63%
is a housing giant: Zillow, and Compass.&nbsp;
And, it seems like Zillow is losing. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:25.680 position:63%
war may shape the future of the housing market,&nbsp;
altogether. Whether it stays open and searchable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:38.000 position:63%
or is controlled by the largest brokerages.
But first, what does Zillow do? Imagine you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.280 position:63%
a real estate agent looking to sell houses. You&nbsp;
need buyers, so you turn to Zillow and pay to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:48.240 position:63%
connect with people looking to buy a home. When&nbsp;
a buyer ends up making a purchase, Zillow takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:54.000 position:63%
a cut of your commission. Which can be as high&nbsp;
as 40%. This "agent business" alone accounted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:01.600 position:63%
roughly two-thirds of Zillow's $2.58 billion in&nbsp;
revenue in 2025. So, pretty important. But Zillow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.440 position:63%
doesn’t own anything. Not any houses, at least,&nbsp;
and not even really the house listings either.&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.240 position:63%
The listings, the photos, the property&nbsp;
details mostly come from real estate agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.040 position:63%
and brokerages. Zillow gets access to these&nbsp;
listings from regional databases and major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.920 position:63%
real estate companies. Zillow's entire business&nbsp;
depends on having homes for people to browse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.120 position:63%
The more listings it has, the more buyers it&nbsp;
attracts. And the more buyers it attracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.480 position:63%
the more valuable it becomes to agents, who&nbsp;
bring money. So, what if this access is affected?&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.800 position:63%
But before we can get to that, we need&nbsp;
to talk about Zillow’s opponent: Compass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.440 position:63%
the real estate brokerage platform, which was&nbsp;
trying to do exactly this. Even though Compass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.600 position:63%
started as a single real estate brokerage,&nbsp;
it has expanded over the years by acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:51.120 position:63%
its rivals. In January 2026, it completed&nbsp;
its acquisition of Anywhere Real Estate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.440 position:63%
the company behind major brands like Coldwell&nbsp;
Banker, Sotheby's International Realty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:02.880 position:63%
and Corcoran. This gave it access to a network of&nbsp;
roughly 340,000 agents, handling around 24% of US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:10.880 position:63%
home sales by value. And in some cities, its reach&nbsp;
can be over 35%. Still, Compass CEO Robert Reffkin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.720 position:63%
has set an ambitious goal to capture 30% of the&nbsp;
market in each of the company's top 30 cities.&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.120 position:63%
And Compass is using its growing reach to&nbsp;
change where homes are listed. Even Redfin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.720 position:63%
one of Zillow's biggest competitors,&nbsp;
agreed to display Compass listings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.480 position:63%
potentially adding more than 500,000 homes&nbsp;
to its platform. But Compass went further.&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.520 position:63%
“On May 8, Compass terminated all direct&nbsp;
listing feed agreements with Zillow nationwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.240 position:63%
on behalf of itself and every Compass&nbsp;
brokerage entity or subsidiary.”&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.680 position:63%
It ended its direct listing feeds to Zillow&nbsp;
across all its brands. Yeah, Zillow wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.480 position:63%
happy about this. In New York, it also&nbsp;
offered agents a $1,000 marketing package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.880 position:63%
to move listings away from StreetEasy,&nbsp;
Zillow's rental and real estate platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:01.520 position:63%
which attracts around two million visitors a&nbsp;
month. So why? Well, on September 22, 2026, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.040 position:63%
company relaunched Compass.com under the slogan&nbsp;
"See It First". This will include properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.640 position:63%
that haven't yet reached the open market. The&nbsp;
strategy is already drawing attention. Compass.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.800 position:63%
visits in Chicago were reportedly up 111%.
It was clear what Compass was doing, and Zillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:24.160 position:63%
was fairly furious about it. It's called a&nbsp;
“private exclusive” strategy. Instead of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.360 position:63%
on a shared listing database other agents use, or&nbsp;
on public sites like Zillow, a private exclusive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.560 position:63%
is a home for sale Compass only shows to its&nbsp;
own agents and their clients. This was part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.760 position:63%
Compass’s three-phase marketing plan. Show homes&nbsp;
privately within their network, then list as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:46.160 position:63%
“coming soon” publicly, then on the open market.
But it comes with some serious trade-offs. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.840 position:63%
not for Compass. On Compass.com, these listings&nbsp;
can also appear without days-on-market counts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.640 position:63%
or the history of price cuts. It means buyers&nbsp;
have less of the full picture. You can’t see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.520 position:63%
if a property is struggling to sell, has been&nbsp;
on the market for ages, and so on. For buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.000 position:63%
homes become harder to find when listings&nbsp;
are scattered across private networks.&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.440 position:63%
And for sellers, ironically,&nbsp;
the cost could be even higher.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:14.320 position:63%
Zillow and Compass both want you searching&nbsp;
with them alone, and that fight has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.720 position:63%
spread to AI. In October 2025, Zillow put&nbsp;
its listings inside ChatGPT. But AI companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.080 position:63%
play the same game. They want you to pick one&nbsp;
model, and stay there. But, you don’t have to.&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.120 position:63%
Models are all great at different things,&nbsp;
search listings with ChatGPT, have Claude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.400 position:63%
review the many PDFs and documents, get Gemini&nbsp;
to organise your open houses in Google Sheets.&nbsp;

