00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:07.200 position:63%
A study of more than one million sales found&nbsp;
that homes listed publicly sold for 17.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.560 position:63%
more than comparable off-market homes.&nbsp;
Why? Because a private listing reaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.080 position:63%
only one agency’s clients. Public listings&nbsp;
have more eyes, more buyers, more offers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:25.120 position:63%
and even bidding wars, which increase prices.
So why would agents want this? Well, there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.000 position:63%
pretty huge upside. According to Zillow's analysis&nbsp;
of Compass data, which you should take with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.040 position:63%
grain of salt, private listings were 72% more&nbsp;
likely to be “double-ended,” meaning the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.320 position:63%
brokerage could collect commission from both&nbsp;
the buyer and seller. And this phenomenon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.120 position:63%
growing quickly. Five years ago, fewer than 1 in&nbsp;
5 sellers said their agent suggested going with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:52.000 position:63%
a private listing. Now, it’s almost 2 in 3.
But Compass disagrees. As CEO Reffkin put it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:57.120 position:63%
“sellers will always have the freedom to choose&nbsp;
how their listings are marketed and sold.” All of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:04.000 position:63%
this was bad news for Zillow. Very bad, but also&nbsp;
potentially for both buyers and sellers. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.880 position:63%
we need a bit more context behind these&nbsp;
moves, and how Zillow might have, ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.560 position:63%
caused this. Because their intentions aren’t&nbsp;
noble either. Let's have a look at the strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:24.640 position:63%
legal battle between Zillow and Compass.
Here, we have two business models that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:30.800 position:63%
can’t coexist. Zillow wants every listing to be on&nbsp;
Zillow. It’s their whole premise, really. Buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.840 position:63%
visit Zillow expecting to find every available&nbsp;
home. And when someone inquires about a property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:40.320 position:63%
Zillow can turn that interest into a lead&nbsp;
for a real estate agent. So, every missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.440 position:63%
listing is a potential buyer who might take their&nbsp;
search, and eventual house sale, somewhere else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.160 position:63%
Zillow's own internal strategy documents&nbsp;
described the risk of this "flywheel effect,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.640 position:63%
in which its addressable market could shrink&nbsp;
if other brokerages followed Compass's lead.&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.360 position:63%
Meanwhile, Compass is doubling down on&nbsp;
showing homes to its own clients first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.560 position:63%
before opening them up to everyone else. Compass&nbsp;
also argues that public price histories and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.720 position:63%
number of days a home spends on the market can&nbsp;
work against sellers. And to be fair, it does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.320 position:63%
but it’s great for buyers is what I’d argue.&nbsp;
Regardless, you have an open model, Zillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:23.520 position:63%
and a closed model, Compass, but both want all the&nbsp;
agents and listings. One of them had to give. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:29.360 position:63%
Zillow decided to make the first move.
In April 2025, Zillow updated its policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.160 position:63%
If you publicly market a house but don't make&nbsp;
it available on Zillow within one business day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.160 position:63%
your listing could be banned from&nbsp;
the platform. You'd get two warnings.&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:41.600 position:63%
“You're going to need to make a choice&nbsp;
in how you want to market a listing.”&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.640 position:63%
After this policy, at Compass, the share of its&nbsp;
agents using the private-first strategy fell from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:53.840 position:63%
39% to 22% in just three months. That’s a huge&nbsp;
drop. Compass, naturally, was pretty unhappy about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:00.160 position:63%
this. So, they sued. But with little evidence&nbsp;
to prove that Zillow operated a monopoly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:06.560 position:63%
in February, the judge refused to block Zillow's&nbsp;
policy. At this point Zillow looked untouchable.&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.800 position:63%
But here’s when Zillow did something... very&nbsp;
ironic. Just weeks after this win, Zillow launched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:18.080 position:63%
Zillow Preview, a product that let participating&nbsp;
agencies showcase homes on Zillow before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:24.320 position:63%
reached the open market... Yes. Really. ... Many&nbsp;
in the real estate world could not believe this.&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:28.240 position:63%
"It's bizzaro world to me that&nbsp;
we're back in the same place again."&nbsp;

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:32.400 position:63%
“A sincere thank you to Zillow for&nbsp;
offering homeowners more choice.”&nbsp;

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.520 position:63%
Still, Zillow defended this choice. Its argument&nbsp;
was that Preview listings were visible to anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.480 position:63%
browsing Zillow, while Compass's private&nbsp;
exclusives were kept off the wider web. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:48.240 position:63%
is true, but many saw a different problem. Rather&nbsp;
than letting Compass decide who sees a home first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.480 position:63%
Zillow wanted to control where buyers&nbsp;
went to find it. Zillow wasn't against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.040 position:63%
early access… It just was against early&nbsp;
access that didn't run through Zillow.&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:02.000 position:63%
But Compass wasn't done. It changed its&nbsp;
strategy. Compass dropped its lawsuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.400 position:63%
Instead of fighting Zillow's rules directly,&nbsp;
it went after something much more important:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:12.080 position:63%
the supply of listings Zillow depends on.&nbsp;
Remember Redfin? A major Zillow competitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:18.240 position:63%
it became a major outlet for Compass homes.
But the bigger move came on April 24. Compass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:23.760 position:63%
partnered with MRED, the massive Chicago-area&nbsp;
database that local agents use to share property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.440 position:63%
listings. A market where Compass handles roughly&nbsp;
35% of home sales. Under the deal, Compass would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:35.200 position:63%
send MRED all its listings, including private&nbsp;
exclusives, and pay for up to 100,000 agents to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.840 position:63%
join the service. In return, MRED promised&nbsp;
to protect participating agents from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:45.280 position:63%
“banned or penalized by third-party portals.”&nbsp;
Wonder which third party they’re talking about.&nbsp;

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:50.080 position:63%
Buried in this arrangement was a rule that could&nbsp;
put Zillow's policy in a tough spot. MRED's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:55.200 position:63%
licensing terms prohibit platforms from filtering&nbsp;
listings based on which brokerage they come from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:01.200 position:63%
This would be disastrous for Zillow. So, they&nbsp;
sued Compass back. Which led to a very strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:12.400 position:63%
chain of events, and how just 9 listings,&nbsp;
caused tens of thousands to disappear.&nbsp;

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:15.920 position:63%
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.640 position:63%
and means you won’t miss future videos.
Zillow wasn't going to let this slide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:25.920 position:63%
They sued Compass and MRED, accusing them of&nbsp;
working together to force Compass's private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:32.160 position:63%
listings onto Zillow. The lawsuit pointed to two&nbsp;
statements in particular. MRED's CEO allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.040 position:63%
said she “was not backing down when it came to&nbsp;
cutting off Zillow's listing feed.” Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:42.160 position:63%
Reffkin allegedly wrote that the “MLS must&nbsp;
discipline Zillow for its new rules.” Zillow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.360 position:63%
argued that it was being forced into a corner to&nbsp;
either abandon its listing policy or risk losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.120 position:63%
access to Chicago's massive property&nbsp;
database. But suing didn't stop MRED.&nbsp;

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:59.840 position:63%
Remember, Compass had sent its private listings&nbsp;
into MRED's database. Under MRED's rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.720 position:63%
those homes counted as MRED listings, and&nbsp;
Zillow couldn't simply pick which ones to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:11.200 position:63%
display. So MRED gave Zillow an ultimatum: display&nbsp;
the nine listings that started private on Compass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:16.880 position:63%
which it had blocked under its new policy,&nbsp;
by 11:59 p.m. on May 19, or lose access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.560 position:63%
every MRED listing. And to make things worse,&nbsp;
none of those nine homes were even in Chicago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:28.800 position:63%
They were in California, Florida, and Georgia.
Zillow refused. By the next morning, thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.160 position:63%
of Zillow's Chicago listings disappeared.&nbsp;
MRED accused Zillow of sacrificing access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:40.080 position:63%
to 43,000 listings just to preserve its right&nbsp;
to exclude nine homes. Meanwhile, on Zillow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.520 position:63%
buyers and sellers were now facing a much worse&nbsp;
housing market than they had the day before.&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:50.720 position:63%
Then the judge stepped in. He ordered MRED&nbsp;
to restore Zillow's access while the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.160 position:63%
continued. But on one condition: Zillow had to&nbsp;
display those nine Compass homes and could no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:01.600 position:63%
longer enforce its ban in MRED's territory. The&nbsp;
judge also barred Zillow from banning listings in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:07.920 position:63%
ZIP codes nationwide where MRED had carried&nbsp;
listings between April 2025 and April 2026.&nbsp;

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:13.120 position:63%
In a hearing in early July, Zillow’s CFO warned&nbsp;
that losing listings could trigger a “downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:18.400 position:63%
spiral.” Why? Well, fewer homes would attract&nbsp;
fewer buyers, making Zillow less valuable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:23.680 position:63%
the agents who paid it for leads. Reffkin argued&nbsp;
that homes left on the open market too long could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:28.880 position:63%
“look like damaged goods.” Reffkin also said&nbsp;
that, according to Compass's internal data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:34.400 position:63%
pre-marketed homes sold for 2.9% more&nbsp;
and went under contract 20% faster.&nbsp;

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:39.600 position:63%
So what happened? Well, fascinatingly, the&nbsp;
judge acknowledged this would cause harm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:45.520 position:63%
to Zillow… then denied their injunction.
"Zillow has presented no evidence of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.760 position:63%
'downward spiral'" in its business&nbsp;
as a result of MRED's actions.&nbsp;

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:54.000 position:63%
Zillow “has not sufficiently connected those&nbsp;
injuries to a reduction in competition,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:58.400 position:63%
and “transparency is not, standing&nbsp;
alone, a goal of the antitrust laws.”&nbsp;

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:01.600 position:63%
The judge, also, concluded that&nbsp;
Zillow could avoid the damage by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:06.240 position:63%
simply not enforcing its listing ban.
Zillow now, is essentially forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.720 position:63%
ignore its rule. And, things continue&nbsp;
to get worse... for Zillow at least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:15.920 position:63%
Compass has signed similar deals with three more&nbsp;
listing databases, including Bright MLS, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:21.760 position:63%
largest in the country. Nashville's Realtracs has&nbsp;
already used that leverage, naming Zillow its only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:27.920 position:63%
non-compliant vendor. Now, Zillow might have put&nbsp;
their control over the real estate market at risk.&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:33.200 position:63%
Zillow has lost roughly two-thirds of its market&nbsp;
value, while Compass is now worth more. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:39.360 position:63%
Zillow’s revenue grew 18% in the second quarter&nbsp;
of 2026, and private listings still make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:46.160 position:63%
only around 1% to 2% of housing inventory. But the&nbsp;
bigger loss is harder to measure. Zillow is losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:51.440 position:63%
control over the listings. It can’t force people&nbsp;
into not showing private listings elsewhere.&nbsp;

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:57.120 position:63%
But, I’m unsure if this outcome is good for&nbsp;
buyers. With Compass, more homes could sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:02.400 position:63%
behind brokerage gates, shown first to insiders&nbsp;
and selected buyers. Senator Elizabeth Warren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:07.280 position:63%
has warned of a “two-tiered housing market&nbsp;
where insiders pay for exclusive access.” But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:12.640 position:63%
early access could increasingly happen through&nbsp;
Zillow itself, via products like Preview. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:17.440 position:63%
housing market might have changed for good.
Did you know that Zillow had an even bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:22.800 position:63%
disaster, just a few years ago? Zillow tried&nbsp;
to escape all this by buying houses itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:35.200 position:63%
And it almost destroyed their entire business.&nbsp;
